  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Front remains stalled offshore to the S of the\nFA. Convection overnight has been blossoming along the boundary,\nand this will likely continue through the day today. Surface \nflow becomes more onshore latter half of the day with the\nseabreeze, kicking off a period of increasing moisture and \ninstability tomorrow onwards across the Southeast U.S. coastline\nwith a modest uptick of shower and thunderstorm coverage as a \nwave of low pressure meandering over GA along the boundary\nnudges the front toward the coast after sunset tonight.\nExpecting a dry day for most of the area though a generous\n15-20% PoP has been kept from previous forecast iterations in\ntypical seabreeze convergence zones over the Albemarle\nPeninsula and Crystal Coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The low pressure currently over SECONUS is \nforecast to lift along the stalled front tomorrow into Friday, \npushing the boundary north into ENC and providing a better \nfocal point for more widespread coverage of showers and storms \nahead of a second front which will cross the area by Sat. Given \nthe multiple days of onshore flow, PWATs do return back closer \nto 2\" tomorrow onwards. With enough instability in place, and\nsubtly increasing of shear, a few isolated stronger storms \naren't completely out of the question for FRI. In addition to \nthis, any shower or thunderstorm that develops will be an \nefficient rainmaker, potentially bringing torrential rainfall \nand a low end risk for flash flooding, mainly in urban and poor \ndrainage areas. As such, SPC has added a Marginal Risk of severe\ntstorms and WPC has upgraded to a Slight Risk of excessive\nrainfall for FRI while the FA is warm sectored with the front \nthat has been offshore then being stalled along or near the \nAppMts. Rain continues into the weekend when a front pushed from\nthe Great Lakes while cross the FA on SAT. High pressure builds\nback in behind SAT's front on SUN, bringing a return back to \nmore climatological precip chances along the seabreeze. Temps\nthrough the weekend remain seasonable with highs in the 80s and\nlows in the mid 60s to mid 70s. Heat returns early next week\nwith low 90/mid 70s splits.",
